```
KEY POINTS:
- model = database table defined as Python class
- class Post(models.Model) = one database table
- Django auto adds 'id' field to every model
- CharField = short text, must have max_length
- TextField = long text, no max_length needed
- IntegerField = whole numbers
- DecimalField = precise decimals (use for money)
- BooleanField = True/False only
- DateTimeField auto_now_add=True = set once on create, never changes
- DateTimeField auto_now=True = updates every time object saved
- null=True = database stores NULL (empty in DB)
- blank=True = form validation allows empty
- use both together for optional fields
- unique=True = no duplicate values allowed
- default= = value used when not provided
- __str__ = controls how object shows in admin/shell
- makemigrations = writes down changes to migration file
- migrate = applies changes to actual database
- always run both in order — every time models.py changes
- Post.objects.create() = create + save in one step
- p1.save() = save object to database
```

---

```python
# QUICK REFERENCE CODE

# blog/models.py
from django.db import models

class Post(models.Model):
    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)
    # short text — max 200 chars

    content = models.TextField()
    # long text — unlimited

    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)
    # set once on create — never changes

    updated_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now=True)
    # updates every time object saved

    is_published = models.BooleanField(default=False)
    # True/False — starts unpublished

    views_count = models.IntegerField(default=0)
    # whole number — starts at 0

    author_name = models.CharField(max_length=100, default="Anonymous")
    # optional with default

    category = models.CharField(max_length=50, null=True, blank=True)
    # optional — null in DB, blank in form

    def __str__(self):
        return self.title
        # shows title in admin/shell instead of <Post object>


# MIGRATIONS — run every time models.py changes
# python manage.py makemigrations
# python manage.py migrate

# SHELL — test your model
# python manage.py shell

from blog.models import Post

p1 = Post.objects.create(title="First Post", content="Hello Django")
# create + save in one step

p2 = Post(title="Second Post", content="Learning Models")
p2.save()
# create then save separately

print(Post.objects.all())
# all posts from database
```

